            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a moderate amount of air leakage, and there are some key areas where I found air leakage.

 

I recommend you outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 



I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed. 



Air leakage was observed along the baseboards and around the window in the bedroom. I recommend that you add some caulking along the baseboards and insulate and seal behind the window trim before replacing it.  



Because your house has the potential to become underventilated if you do a lot of air sealing work, I have included information about heat recovery ventilation later in this report. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $90 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$500 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation with 2x2 interior strapping with no insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



When you add exterior insulation, extend it all the way down over the header and the top part of your basement walls that are currently covered with siding. This upgrade is discussed later and the savings listed here do not include this portion of the walls. 



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $280 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 8.0 GJ, and qualify for up to$2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of your house was assumed to have R28 blown fiberglass insulation. As you mentioned you may be interested in adding more insulation, I have modelled adding enough blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50.



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 3.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $125 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be wood framed with 2x6 framing, R20 fibreglass batt insulation, and then 2x2 strapping with no insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene, insulation to the exterior of your basement walls down to the ground as well as the header between the basement and the main floor of your house



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the above ground portion of your basement walls, you could potentially save $230 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $416.88 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for basement insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a basement insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass, I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all your windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



This upgrade is best done at the same time as an exterior insulation upgrade. 



Savings: 

By replacing all your windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $250 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 7.2 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

Your house is not currently underventilated, but it has the potential to become underventilated if you do a significant amount of air sealing work. Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



I recommend that you consider having an ENERGY STAR?????? rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. An HRV could be tied into the existing furnace duct work to minimize installation requirements.



Installing an HRV will increase your energy costs slightly, but will help ensure that you have a safe and comfortable indoor environment, and help protect the air quality inside your home. 



Costs:

By installing an ENERGY STAR?????? rated HRV, you could potentially spend an additional $110 per year in energy costs, and increase your annual energy usage by 2.2 GJ. There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 83% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



I also recommend that you have a qualified heating contractor install an electronic damper on your combustion air duct to ensure that combustion air is only coming in the room when the boiler or hot water tank is operating. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $520 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 13.5 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



PELLET STOVE

As you are planning on replacing your pellet stove, I have modelled the potential savings of using it for 25% of your heating load. AEA recommends a ULC Certified pellet stove. Ensure that the installation complies with local building and fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct.



Savings:

By installing a certified pellet stove and using it for 25% of your heating load, you could potentially save $330 per year in energy savings, and qualify for up to 1/2 of pre-tax purchase cost of the stove to a maximum of $1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh

Oil  - $1.27 per litre 

Propane - $0.865 per litre

Wood - $315.00 per cord 

Pellets - $7.35 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

